/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  Reversible water-gas-shift kinetics
      CO + H2O  <-->  CO2 + H2

  Forward rate constant from Singh & Saraf (1977) for a Fe/Cr HT-WGS
  catalyst, fitted in pseudo-homogeneous form for the empty-volume
  rate definition the CSTR uses.  Mass-action orders (1 each).

  The `reversible true;` flag tells the CSTR to derive k_rev from
  k_fwd / K_eq, with
       K_eq(T) = exp(-dG_rxn(T) / RT)
       dG_rxn(T) = g_CO2 + g_H2 - g_CO - g_H2O  (all g_pure_ig)

  At T = 600 K, K_eq ~ 26.5; equilibrium conversion of an equimolar
  CO + H2O feed (1 + 1, V_R = 1e-2 m^3) ~ 84 % (matches the textbook
  Smith-Van Ness Le Chatelier value).

  THE FEED IS A GAS, AND THE PFR PRICES ITS CONCENTRATIONS ON THE GAS (DEV.md
  4c C421): the feed is declared `phase gas;`, so Q = F R T / P from the
  case's equation of state, exactly as the CSTR sibling does.  Until C421 the
  unit priced this gas on its components' stored 25 C LIQUID volumes (Q about
  186 times too small, concentrations that large), and A below was chosen
  small for that reason.  A is now 1.0e6 (see below) and V_R 1e-2 m^3.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

wgs_reversible
{
    limitingReactant   CO;
    reversible         true;

    stoichiometry
    (
        { component CO;    nu -1;  order 1; }
        { component water; nu -1;  order 1; }
        { component CO2;   nu  1;  order 0; }   // reverse-side order = nu (mass action)
        { component H2;    nu  1;  order 0; }
    );

    kinetics
    {
        type   Arrhenius;
        A      1.0e6;         // ILLUSTRATIVE rate: mol/(m^3.s) * (m^3/mol)^2.  Ten times below the CSTR sibling's 1.0e7 because the PFR is
                       // marched by an EXPLICIT RK4: r dV / F must stay below about 2.8 per step, and at 1.0e7 it is 4 (ladder01's
                       // fifth of the feed: 10), the profile then sticks at zero conversion.
                       // The forward A only sets HOW FAST equilibrium is
                       // reached; K_eq -- hence X_eq ~ 0.841, identical to
                       // the CSTR -- is thermodynamic and A-independent.
        Ea     8.0e4;          // J/mol
    }
}
